{"type": "investigation_result", "text": "I couldn't investigate this. The instance and volume IDs you gave don't exist in account 012345678901_1, and no alarm on `VolumeReadBytes` exists there either. I haven't found a cause.

*What I checked (account 012345678901_1, us-east-1):*
• *Instance and volume:* EC2 rejected both IDs as invalid (`InvalidInstanceID.Malformed` for `i-0123456789abcdef0_1`, `InvalidParameterValue` for `vol-0123456789abcdef0`). These look like the example IDs from AWS documentation rather than real ones.
• *The alarm:* No alarm watches `AWS/EBS VolumeReadBytes` in this account. The only alarms in ALARM are four DynamoDB auto-scaling alarms on the `Instancestopstart-*` tables, stuck in that state since 2019-02-20, which have nothing to do with this.
• *What does exist:* There are 14 gp3 volumes, none with those IDs. Only two instances are running: `i-0123456789abcdef0_9` (m5.xlarge) and `i-0123456789abcdef0_2` (t3.medium, with gp3 volume `vol-0123456789abcdef0_17`). I haven't assumed either is your batch host.

*To continue, please send:*
1. The real instance ID and volume ID.
2. The region, if it isn't us-east-1, and the account, if it isn't 012345678901_1.
3. The exact alarm name or ARN.

*What I'll check once I have them:* Your description (read bytes high, IOPS normal) fits the volume hitting its throughput limit rather than its IOPS limit. That happens when the job reads in large blocks. Default gp3 is 3,000 IOPS but only 125 MiB/s, so 3,000 reads of 256 KiB each would need about 750 MiB/s. I'll compare:
• read bytes against the volume's throughput setting;
• the average read size (read bytes ÷ read operations);
• queue length and read latency;
• the instance type's own EBS bandwidth limit;
• any recent volume changes or job changes from about an hour ago."}
